 "findings": [
  "100 ms per hop (d100) is at chance over 3 runs (10.8%, 95% interval 8.7-12.9%) and 32% faster than 200 ms (median 1.71-1.78 s vs 2.58-2.64 s), at the same bandwidth.",
  "50 ms per hop scored 13.5% in its one run; its 95% interval (9.8-17.3%) still includes chance, so one run cannot tell. It is not recommended without repeats.",
  "Halving the cover rate with 100 ms delays (d100half) halves the upload (~290 kbit/s) but leaks: 12.8% over 3 runs, its 95% interval above 10%. Its first run alone (11.0%) looked safe.",
  "Doubling the send rate (p2) buys 14% latency for 66% more bandwidth.",
  "Compression end to end: kernel.org arrives whole with gzip (20,436-byte page) and does not fit one reply without it (502).",
  "Ten cover clients (same software and workload, unscored): an attacker who cannot tell them from users is at chance even at 50 ms (6.4% against 5%); one who can (enumerable generators) is not at 50 ms (15.2%, interval 11.0-19.4% against 10%) and is at 100 ms (12.3%, interval 8.4-16.2%). Cover lets the delay shrink only if it is indistinguishable from users where the adversary watches. One run each."
